  "shared_denominator_artifact_confirmed": true,
  "explanation": "The per-row coupling that a naive analysis sees is real for one pair and entirely arithmetic for the other. (i) LSR vs |grad_d18o|: r=+0.468 (n=547), which looks like strong sedimentation control on proxy variability, is an artifact of the shared denominator. LSR=np.gradient(depth,age) and grad_d18o=np.gradient(d18o,age) both divide a between-row difference by the SAME age increment -- the per-row step dAge=np.gradient(age) -- so within each row the two gradients are two numerators scaled by one common factor. Evidence: the position-wise mean |grad_perm| of pure-noise surrogates, an estimate of the deterministic amplitude channel that depends only on the age grid, already correlates with LSR at r=+0.972 against the observed r=+0.468. Permutation confirms it: with d18o values shuffled in age order (n_perm=2000), the null r(LSR,|grad|) = +0.468 +/- 0.037 (sd) versus observed +0.468, two-sided p=0.50 (z=+0.01) - pure noise, with no process, reproduces the entire correlation. Dividing |grad| by that position-wise amplitude drops it to +0.131 (residual p=0.0055, i.e. ~7.8% of the naive shared variance). (ii) The LINEAR partial correlation controlling for dAge is +0.391, i.e. it does NOT collapse, because the artifact is proportional rather than additive: the scale factor sits inside a product, so regressing out dAge linearly cannot remove it (partial given 1/dAge, +0.399, likewise fails). The permutation null and the amplitude correction are the valid tests; conditioning on dAge is not. Note dAge here (np.gradient(age), range 0.20-2.53 ka) is the age increment itself; np.gradient(age, age) is identically 1.0 and carries no information. (iii) LSR vs the d18o LEVEL shares no denominator and is not an artifact: r=-0.407 (n=593) against a permutation null of -0.001 +/- 0.041, two-sided p=0.0004998. The decisive block test agrees: block-mean LSR vs block-mean d18o gives Pearson -0.508 (n_blocks=35), -0.500 (n_blocks=18) and -0.743 (n_blocks=9) at 10, 20 and 40 ka (Spearman -0.462/-0.496/-0.283; block-level permutation p=0.00145/0.0345/0.0217), all negative and sign-stable under leave-one-block-out (ranges [-0.544,-0.475], [-0.573,-0.404], [-0.838,-0.568]) - no single interval drives it. So accumulation rate and proxy LEVEL are coupled at inter-block scale (higher LSR goes with lighter d18O), and that coupling survives the null that kills the gradient-based one; the |grad_d18o| version of the coupling is a shared-denominator artifact and must not be reported as process evidence. Robustness: shifting the bin edges through a full width at 10/20/40 ka keeps every refit negative (10/10 shifts each; 10 ka range [-0.508,-0.426], 20 ka [-0.579,-0.396], 40 ka [-0.754,-0.292]), and the block-mean correlation is not a shared age trend - partialling out block age leaves it essentially unchanged at 10 ka (-0.508 -> -0.510) and 20 ka (-0.500 -> -0.503). The coupling is also scale dependent: it holds from 5 to 25 ka and breaks down at 30 ka (r=+0.094), 50-60 ka and 100 ka where only 4-12 blocks remain, so the apparent strength at 80 ka (r=-0.851) rests on 5 points and is not credible. Caveats: block means of LSR and d18o both co-vary with age over the 2.8-359 ka span of a single core, so the inter-block coupling is a within-record association that cannot separate a true accumulation-rate control from a shared response to age-dependent glacial-interglacial state; the 40 ka Spearman (-0.283) is much weaker than its Pearson (-0.743) with only 9 blocks, so the 10-20 ka result carries the weight. The 28 missing d18o values were left as missing rather than imputed; they thin the record but do not create the pattern.",
